#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:6
msgid "New Environments from Scratch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:8
msgid ""
"Users in the EaaSI Network are encouraged to make new environments using "
"the :ref:`emulation-project` menu and the existing corpus of pre-"
"configured :term:`base` environments published to the Network to reduce "
"redundant dependencies and troubleshooting. However, this is not an "
"acceptable solution for self-hosted EaaSI users outside of the EaaSI "
"Network or another consortial partnership."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:10
msgid ""
"Functionality to create new environments completely from scratch is under"
" development for the Emulation Project interface with expected release "
"date in the first half of 2023. In the meantime, EaaSI users can create "
"new environments using the follow work-around, temporarily accessing "
"their installation's Demo UI at ``<https://<your-eaasi-url>/admin``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:13
msgid "Import Bootable Object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:15
msgid ""
"Assuming you have not done so already: to create a new environment, you "
"must first obtain a piece of bootable software (e.g. installation media) "
"and upload it to EaaSI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:17
msgid ""
"Log in using your same Admin user credentials at ``https://<your-eaasi-"
"url>/admin>``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:19
msgid "Navigate to the \"Objects\" menu, then click on \"Import Object\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:23
msgid ""
"Name and then use the \"Add File\" menu to select your bootable software,"
" including the appropriate Media Type for the object file(s) (Floppy, "
"ISO, or arbitrary set of Files). Here is an example configuration using "
"the ISO installation media for FreeDOS 1.2:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:27
msgid ""
"Select \"Import\" and allow Emulation-as-a-Service to complete the object"
" upload."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:29
msgid ""
"**Optional**: If your bootable object is installation media, you may wish"
" to use the Demo UI to further classify your uploaded object both as a "
":term:`Software` resource and as an operating system installer. The "
"object can be used to run and create a new Environment *either way*; "
"marking an object as Software and an operating system installer just "
"helps organize your resources and can help you find your bootable object "
"more quickly in a later step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:31
msgid ""
"To do so, on the \"Objects\" page, find your just-uploaded bootable "
"object under the \"Local Object Archive\" tab and select \"Details\" from"
" the \"Choose Action\" dropdown. First check the box that says \"Object "
"is software\", then, when the \"Software Properties\" section appears, "
"expand it and check the box that says \"Is an operating system\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:37
msgid "Create Blank Image"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:39
msgid ""
"This step is optional if you only wish to emulate a \"live\" computing "
"environment (i.e. run from temporary, bootable media like a Linux Live "
"CD). However, assuming you would like to actually install a fresh "
"operating system and/or have some persistent storage available in your "
"new environment, it is recommended to follow these steps to create a "
"blank disk image to install your bootable system on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:41
msgid "Select \"New Image\" on the Images menu:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:45
msgid ""
"On the \"Image Management\" modal, select the \"Create new image\" radio "
"button, and select a size for the new (blank) disk image under \"New Disk"
" Disk Size\", using MB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:47
msgid ""
"Also give the new disk image a descriptive label (blank disk images can "
"be re-used for installing/creating multiple environments later):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:51
msgid ""
"Select \"OK\" and wait for Emulation-as-a-Service to finish creating the "
"new, blank disk image. We will now use this blank disk image to create a "
"new environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:54
msgid "Create New Environment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:56
msgid ""
"Navigate to the \"Environment\" section of the Demo UI, then select "
"\"Create Environment\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:60
msgid ""
"Select the general \"family\" of operating systems that you would like to"
" install or run in your new environment:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:64
msgid ""
"Name the Environment you want to create, then use the \"Select operating "
"system/System\" dropdown menu to select the closest equivalent of your "
"specific operating system. These options are templates that will auto-"
"populate your Environment with an appropriate :term:`hardware "
"configuration`. (To have templates in this menu, you must have imported "
"Emulator images first; see :ref:`Managing Emulators <managing_emulators>`"
" and :ref:`emulator compatility <emulators>` for process and "
"recommendations)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:68
msgid ""
"Scroll down to the \"Drive Settings\" section. If you will be installing "
"an operating system from scratch in the new environment, click on the "
"drive labeled \"**Drive Type**: disk empty drive, boot drive\" (should be"
" the third drive in the list)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:72
msgid ""
"Under \"Drive Settings\", click the \"Select from disk image library\" "
"radio button and use the dropdown menu to select the blank disk image "
"created earlier:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:76
msgid ""
"Click OK to save the image selection for that drive. Now find the first "
"appropriate drive type to match the bootable object you uploaded earlier "
"(e.g. the first \"floppy empty drive\" if it was a Floppy-type object; "
"\"cdrom empty drive\" if it was either an ISO or Files-type object)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:78
msgid ""
"Again, using that drive's settings menu, use one of the dropdown menus as"
" appropriate to find and select your bootable object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../guide/make_new_environment.rst:80
msgid ""
"(If you marked the object as Software, it should be available under the "
"\"software library\" dropdown; if you marked it as an operating system it"
" should be available under the \"operating-system media library\"; if you"
" did neither, the object should still be available under the \"user "
